{
  "id": "https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/obstetric-violence",
  "type": "Concept",
  "prefLabel": {
    "en": "obstetric violence"
  },
  "definition": {
    "en": "Over-medicalisation mistreatment and violence experienced by women in reproductive health services during pregnancy, childbirth, and postpartum."
  },
  "scopeNote": {
    "en": [
      "The term, which emerged in Latin America, covers interventions without consent –caesareans, episiotomies, inductions or unnecessary examinations –, dehumanising or humiliating treatment, lack of information and privacy, or the use of coercive measures. The Special Rapporteur on violence against women described it as a form of gender-based violence and a human rights violation, rooted in stereotypes and power imbalances within health care, and the CEDAW Committee expressly links it to the absence of informed consent and to discrimination through gender stereotypes. It particularly affects women with disabilities, exposed to forced treatments and to barriers of accessibility and understandable information; hence the requirement of free and informed consent and of support in decision-making (arts. 12, 17 and 25 CRPD)."
    ]
  },
  "notation": [
    "13.8.4"
  ],
  "broader": {
    "id": "https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/violence-against-women",
    "prefLabel": {
      "en": "violence against women"
    }
  },
